{0 Parkour}

Parkour is a parser-combinator library for human-authored text: configuration
files, DSLs and small languages. Its aims are error messages that tell the user
what is wrong and where, an API that reads well, opt-in error recovery, and
robustness against hostile input. It depends on nothing beyond the OCaml
standard library.

{1 An example}

{[
open Parkour

let assignment =
  context "assignment"
    (let+ name = take_while1 "identifier" is_letter
     and+ () = skip_while is_space *> char '=' *> cut *> skip_while is_space
     and+ value = integer in
     (name, value))

let () =
  let input = "ammo = banana" in
  match parse assignment input with
  | Ok (name, n) -> Printf.printf "%s = %d\n" name n
  | Error diagnostics ->
      let source = Source.make ~name:"config.txt" input in
      List.iter
        (fun d -> print_string (Diagnostic.render source d))
        diagnostics
]}

prints

{v
error: expected integer, found "banana"
 --> config.txt:1:8
  |
1 | ammo = banana
  |        ^^^^^^ expected integer
  = while parsing assignment, from 1:1
v}

Diagnostics are also plain data ({!Parkour.Diagnostic}: span, expected, found,
context, labels, notes), so editors and other tools can use them directly.

{1 Features}

- {b Error messages.} The failure that got furthest is reported, with what was
  expected and what was found, secondary labels such as "opened here" for
  unclosed brackets, and the constructs being parsed. {!Parkour.label},
  {!Parkour.context}, {!Parkour.hidden}, {!Parkour.fail} and
  {!Parkour.validate} shape the messages.
- {b Explicit commitment.} Choice is ordered and backtracks by default;
  {!Parkour.cut} commits once a construct is recognised and
  {!Parkour.attempt} undoes it.
- {b Error recovery}, opt-in: {!Parkour.recover}, {!Parkour.or_insert},
  {!Parkour.skip_past} and {!Parkour.parse_partial} report many problems in one
  run and still return a partial result.
- {b Lexical conventions.} {!Parkour.Lexeme.make} builds whitespace, comment,
  identifier and keyword parsers for a language; numbers, quoted strings and
  JSON-style escapes are built in.
- {b Token input.} {!Parkour.tokenize} and {!Parkour.Tokens} split parsing into
  a lexer and a grammar over tokens, with diagnostics that still point into the
  original text. {!Parkour.Make} accepts other kinds of input.
- {b Expressions.} {!Parkour.expression} builds operator parsers from a
  precedence table; {!Parkour.chainl1}, {!Parkour.sep_by} and
  {!Parkour.between} cover the usual shapes.
- {b UTF-8.} Diagnostics align underlines for wide and combining characters,
  and {!Parkour.Utf8} parses Unicode scalars, reporting invalid bytes.
- {b Robustness.} Repetitions are loops, so long inputs do not use the stack,
  and nesting is limited ([max_depth]): deeply nested input is a
  [nesting too deep] error, not a stack overflow.

{1 Where to go next}

- {{!page-tutorial}The tutorial} builds parsers step by step, with the error
  output of each step.
- {!Parkour} is the reference for every combinator, including the precise
  semantics of commitment, error merging and recovery.
- The [examples/] directory of the source tree has complete programs, each
  printing a gallery of broken inputs: JSON, CSV, typed values, a calculator, an
  INI-like configuration with recovery, and a small C-like language parsed both
  directly from text and from tokens.
